that sometimes that can be a good thing because people persist in trying to find a good explanation and in the course of doing that they might discover important things about the world. But other times it might lead them to perseverate in trying to find a good explanation when there's just none to be had. And if you buy the idea that explanations in some ways provide us with pleasure, there is also potentially a risk here

66
00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:50.000
because it could be that some explanations, in fact, are more pleasurable than others. In fact, they feel like they fit better. They're more enjoyable to contemplate. And if we're drawn to explanations in part because we derive pleasure from them, it's quite likely that from time to time we're going to draw the conclusion

67
00:33:50.000 --> 00:34:10.000
that the pleasurable explanation is the right explanation when in fact it's not. I think that's right. So in my own work, I sometimes talk about explanatory virtues, which are properties like simplicity that make an explanation good and that often things that we should be seeking in science and everyday life, but we also fall prey to explanatory vices, characteristics of explanations

68
00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:46.000
that are seductive and that draw us in, but maybe aren't really good guides to how we ought to think about the world. How does this this body of work play out when it comes to things like polarization and partisanship? Well, I think explanation is sort of a funny process and that it can lead to two very diametrically opposed consequences. So, typically, when we try to explain something, we're trying to accommodate it in the context of what we already believe. Part of what's going to make it simple and compelling is that it fits in with the assumptions that we're already working with.

69
00:34:46.000 --> 00:35:33.000
